// Comparison of Quick Sort and Merge Sort

// Quick Sort:

Quick Sort is a divide and conquer algorithm, which works by repeatedly dividing a list into two halves until it is sorted. It is a recursive algorithm that has a time complexity of O(n log n) in the average and worst case, but is O(n^2) in the best case. Quick Sort works in place, meaning that it does not need extra memory to store the sorted result.

// Merge Sort:

Merge Sort is a sorting algorithm that uses divide and conquer. It is a recursive algorithm that has a time complexity of O(n log n) in all cases. Merge Sort works by dividing the list into two halves, sorting each halves separately, and then merging them back together. Unlike Quick Sort, Merge Sort needs extra memory to store the sorted result, as it does not work in place.

// Comparison:

Both Quick Sort and Merge Sort are divide and conquer algorithm and have the same time complexity. The difference between the two algorithms lies in its extra memory usage and in place working. Quick Sort does not need extra memory to perform sorting, but it has a worst case time complexity of O(n^2). On the other hand, Merge Sort uses extra memory but has a consistent time complexity of O(n log n). Therefore, Merge Sort is generally more reliable, but Quick Sort may be more efficient in certain scenarios.

